window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][80] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Critique the setup experience of wireless earbuds sold with a phone, and say what you would change.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Pairing should be easier.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the friction removed and the ecosystem effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Labels verified claims, ranks issues including ecosystem dependence and repair, proposes a change with metric and cost, and states the hardware constraint.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general and uses public Apple announcements; no internal claim. &quot;Goal: wireless audio that works without fuss with the user's devices; for the company, hardware sales and ecosystem depth; I judge by the speed and reliability of first connection and later switching. Verified from the company's 2016 announcement: opening the case near an iPhone and tapping sets up the earbuds, and they work with all devices signed into the same iCloud account; in-ear sensors pause and play automatically; the launch slipped from late October to orders on 13 December. Inference: pairing was the biggest friction in earbuds and moving it into the chip and the account made setup the differentiator; the account link raises switching cost. Issues, ranked: first (opinion, high), full value depends on owning the company's devices; second (inference), small and easy to lose, with a battery that wears out; third (observation), a hardware component can move a launch. My change: a clear 'replace one earbud' path with transparent pricing. Metrics: replacement rate, support contacts per 1,000 units and satisfaction at twelve months. Cost: inventory and service operations.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Labels; the removed friction; ranked issues; a cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you decide whether to build a travel mode for a specific segment in a new market, such as motorcyclists in a country with many?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;If there are many riders, build it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sizes the segment and identifies what differs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Identifies the different routing, speed and cue requirements, estimates reach and accuracy risk, uses a staged launch with metrics and safety guardrails, and cites public evidence carefully.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I use public statements about a similar launch and claim no internal knowledge. &quot;Start with the job: what is different for this segment's trips? Public statements about an India-first mode say that riders use shortcuts closed to cars, move differently in traffic and navigate by landmarks <code>[V: press, 2017]</code>. So three things differ: routing rules, travel-time models and guidance cues. Size: the segment's share of trips in the market, from public vehicle statistics and our own data. Risk: accuracy depends on local data (restrictions, shortcuts) and errors cost trust and safety. A staged plan: launch in a few cities where the data is strongest; measure mode adoption among likely riders, the gap between predicted and actual arrival time, and navigation abandonment; guardrails: safety incident reports and distraction (voice guidance). Reach: if the segment is a large share of trips and the baseline error is high, build; if it's small or the data is thin, use a lighter step (a route preference) first.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The job; what differs; sizing; a staged plan with safety.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You are rolling out paid sharing for a subscription service. How do you plan it, and what do you tell leadership to expect?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Roll it out and see what happens.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Stages the rollout and sets measures.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Predicts a cancellation reaction, defines success on net additions and revenue, plans by market, communicates clearly to members, and sets guardrails; uses public precedent carefully.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I use public shareholder letters as precedent and claim no internal knowledge. &quot;Set expectations first: a public letter from a streaming company said it expected a 'cancel reaction' in each market when it rolled out paid sharing, based on an earlier experience in Latin America <code>[V: shareholder letter, January 2023]</code>, and later reported that revenue in each region was higher than before launch and sign-ups exceeded cancellations <code>[V: shareholder letter, July 2023]</code>. I'd tell leadership to expect a short-term dip in net additions, a recovery as extra members and new accounts come in, and to judge success on revenue and net additions over several quarters, not on the first month. &quot;Plan: stage by market, starting where sharing is common and the product is strong; communicate clearly (what changes, what it costs, how to add an extra member); offer an easy way to move a profile to its own account; measure cancellations, sign-ups, extra-member adds, complaint rate and retention of the converted accounts; guardrails: member satisfaction and support contacts. Decision rule: pause and adjust a market if cancellations exceed a threshold set in advance or complaints spike.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Expectations; staging; success on revenue and net additions; communication; guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A messaging app encrypts every message so the company cannot read it. How do you fight spam and abuse?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Scan messages for bad content.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses behavior and reports, not content.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Designs signals from behavior and metadata, user reporting, friction, limits and account-level actions, measures prevalence and errors, and states what cannot be detected.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I use a public account of one platform's experience and claim no internal knowledge. &quot;If I cannot read content, I use behavior. A public account from an engineer at an encrypted messenger reported that spam fell by 75% after encryption was introduced because behavioral signals detect spam without content <code>[V: press reporting a 2017 conference talk]</code>. Signals: message volume and speed, the share of recipients who do not know the sender, how many people block or report the sender, the age and pattern of the account and device, and group-joining patterns. Controls: limits on forwarding and bulk messaging, friction for new accounts, easy blocking and reporting (a reported message can be shared with the company by the recipient), account-level penalties and detection of automation. Measures: prevalence of spam by sampling reports, time to action, appeal rate, and false-positive rate on legitimate users, with a guardrail on friction. &quot;What cannot be done: detecting a harm that exists only in content, such as a specific kind of abusive image, without a user report or a content signal the user chooses to share. For those, reporting and metadata carry more weight, and some harms will be detected later or not at all; I'd say so, and make sure policy and law enforcement processes handle what is reported.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Behavior signals; controls; measures; honesty about limits.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A recommendation feed has high return rates, but you suspect it is narrowing what people see. How do you check, and what do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add more variety.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines a diversity metric and tests an intervention.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures narrowing over time per user, separates choice from the system's effect, tests exploration with guardrails, reads effects by week, and states the tradeoff with engagement.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd define narrowing as a falling variety of what a user is shown and plays over time, compared with their own earlier behavior and a control group. Measures: the number of distinct artists or topics per user per month, the share of plays from the user's top three, and the share of plays of items new to the user. To separate the system's effect from the user's taste, I'd compare users who got personalized lists with a holdout who got a less personalized mix, and look at the change in variety over weeks (popularity bias and feedback loops, C23.8). &quot;If narrowing shows, the intervention is an exploration share (say 10% of the list from outside the user's pattern) with a steer control; test it with the saves-per-listener metric as primary, discovery as a second primary, and return rate as a guardrail. Sample: about 9,800 per arm to detect a two-point rise in saves from a 50% baseline. Read by week for novelty. The tradeoff: exploratory items are skipped more, so short-term engagement may dip; I'd accept a small dip if discovery and long-term return improve, and set the threshold in advance.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Narrowing defined; a holdout; exploration with guardrails; the tradeoff.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A watch notifies a wearer of a possible irregular heart rhythm. Design what happens after the alert.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Tell them to see a doctor.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives clear next steps and handles anxiety.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Designs a guided path using verified study facts, offers confirming options, uses plain language about what the alert means, measures follow-through and distress, and works within regulation and clinical input.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general and uses a published study; no internal claim. &quot;Facts from the published study: 0.52% of participants were notified; the notification had a positive predictive value of 0.84 against a simultaneous monitor patch, but only 34% of notified participants who wore a patch about two weeks later showed the condition, and the authors noted that the condition can come and go, so a later patch can miss it <code>[V: peer-reviewed paper, 2019]</code>. So a person who gets an alert has most likely had a real irregular rhythm, may not show it again soon, and needs to understand both. &quot;Design: plain language ('your watch noticed an irregular rhythm; this is not a diagnosis; it can be worth talking to a clinician'); an immediate choice of a confirming step: record a reading now if the device supports it, book a clinic or telehealth visit, or schedule a monitor; a summary the person can share with a clinician; no alarmist wording and clear urgent-symptom guidance (chest pain, fainting means seek emergency care). Measures: the share who take a confirming step within two weeks, the share who report distress, and the share of alerts followed by a diagnosis (reported with consent); guardrails: false-alert reports and unnecessary visits. Process: clinical review of wording, and regulatory consideration of claims (C27.9).&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Facts used correctly; a guided path; plain language; measures; clinical and regulatory care.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c28-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C28-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C28 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A bold redesign of search, launched as the company's biggest change in a decade, appears to be hurting users who know exactly what they want. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Roll it back.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments by intent and measures the harm.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the inspiration and fixed-intent jobs, measures the cost to each with a holdout, keeps the default path for the common intent, adds the redesigned path alongside, sets a decision rule, and communicates openly.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First separate the jobs: guests who want inspiration and guests who know where and when they want to stay. Measure each: search-to-booking conversion, time to a booking, the number of steps, and complaints, by intent segment, with a holdout on the old search where possible. Public commentary on a similar redesign said it forced searching by category and confused location-bound searchers <code>[V: commentary, 2023; opinion]</code>, and the company redesigned its type-of-place filter in 2023 and, according to trade press, ended category search in 2025 <code>[V: Airbnb release; trade press]</code>. I'd treat those as hypotheses to check in our own data. &quot;If the harm to fixed-intent users is real, restore the classic path as the default and keep the new browsing path as an optional surface for people who want inspiration; test that combination against both groups. Decision rule: keep any element that raises inspiration-segment bookings without lowering fixed-intent conversion beyond a stated margin. Communicate openly to hosts and guests what is changing and why. A bold change that gets adjusted after learning is normal; the mistake is leaving a harmed segment unmeasured.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Intent segments; measured harm; a default path; a decision rule; openness.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a feature factory, and how would you avoid being one?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A team that ships too many features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A team judged by output instead of outcomes, with a fix.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names detection signals (outcome coverage, shipped-and-measured share), explains the root cause, and describes practices with a check.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;A feature factory is a team that is rewarded for shipping features, not for what the features change. It happens because output is visible and outcomes are slow. I'd look for two signals: the share of roadmap items that have an outcome metric and target (if under half, it's a warning), and the share of shipped items that were measured afterward. If 6 of 20 items have metrics and 3 of 14 shipped items were reviewed, the team cannot tell which launches mattered. &quot;To avoid it: every item gets a metric, a baseline, a target and a review date before work begins; I keep an outcomes log; I report outcome coverage and on-time reviews beside velocity; and I stop items whose metric doesn't move after review. The check is a number: in the next quarter I'd want near 100% coverage on new items and over 80% of launches reviewed on time.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The definition; measurable signals; practices with a check.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a vanity metric? Give an example and a better alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A metric that looks good but means nothing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> One that rises without informing decisions, with an example.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives a test (would a decision change if it fell?), an example with the better metric and a guardrail, and notes the role of distributions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;A vanity metric is a number that rises reliably and flatters but does not tell you whether the product is working or what to do. My test is: if this number fell, would any decision change? Registered users is the standard example: it only grows. A better metric is weekly active users who return in each of the last four weeks, by cohort, which shows whether people keep coming back. I'd pair it with a guardrail such as complaints or cancellations so that it cannot be raised by tricks. &quot;Averages can also be vanity: a mean session length of 33 seconds can hide that 90% of sessions are 3 seconds. So I'd report the median and a share-above-threshold, such as the share of users with three meaningful sessions a week.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A decision test; a better metric with a guardrail; distributions.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A senior stakeholder insists you build a feature because a competitor has it. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build it to stay competitive,&quot; or &quot;refuse.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks about the user and the evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates strategy by analogy from a real gap, checks who is leaving and why, sizes the gap, offers a test, and uses a decision rule while respecting the decider.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd take the concern seriously and ask for the evidence. 'They have it' is strategy by analogy; the questions are whether customers are leaving because we lack it, how many, and what they say. I'd check churn reasons, lost-deal notes and usage of the competitor's feature if public, and ask who would use it and what job it does for them. I'd also ask how the competitor's situation differs from ours, and who has tried it and failed. &quot;Then propose a proportional step: a smoke test or a manual version, with a threshold agreed in advance, and a decision date. If the evidence shows a real gap, build it; if not, document why and keep watching. If the stakeholder decides to build it anyway and it's within their authority, I'd commit, write down my concern and the signal that would make us revisit, and measure the result.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence over analogy; a test with a rule; commitment after the decision.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Users keep asking for a feature. Do you build it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, users know what they want.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks about the underlying need.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates requests from situations, counts who is asking and what share they represent, checks behavior, considers alternative solutions, and tests before building.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd treat the request as a symptom of a job, not as a specification. I'd ask: what are they trying to do when they want it, what do they do today, and how often? I'd count how many are asking and what share of users and revenue they are, because a loud ten may not represent a quiet thousand. I'd check behavior: do those users already work around the problem, and where do they drop? &quot;Then I'd list other ways to meet the need (a setting, a default, a different feature) and test the cheapest one: a fake door to see how many click, or a manual version for five customers. I build the request as asked only if it turns out to be the best way to serve the need and the numbers justify it.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Request versus job; counting and representation; alternatives; a cheap test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your launch hit its date, but the metric did not move. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Wait and see,&quot; or &quot;ship another feature.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Checks adoption and measurement.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Diagnoses along the funnel (awareness, activation, value), checks the metric and holdout design, reads effects by week and segment, decides to iterate, stop or extend, and records the learning.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First check that the measurement is sound: is the metric computed correctly, is there a holdout, and is the sample large enough? Then diagnose along the path: did users find the feature (awareness), try it (activation), and get value (repeat use)? If adoption is 4% against a 15% goal, the problem is awareness or activation, with different fixes: placement and messaging, or a simpler first use. If adoption is fine and the metric did not move, the feature may not affect the outcome, and the assumption linking them was wrong. &quot;Read by week and segment: a novelty bump that faded, or an effect in one segment that is diluted in the average. Decide by the criteria set before launch: iterate if there is a fixable path, stop if the assumption failed (and free the capacity), extend the test if it is underpowered. Then record the learning in the outcomes log.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Measurement check; funnel diagnosis; segments and weeks; a decision rule; a record.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you spot your own recurring mistakes?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I reflect after projects.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Reviews artifacts and asks for feedback.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Runs a scan on artifacts with detection signals, counts patterns, finds the root-cause cluster, chooses one practice with a retest, and uses outside examples.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I scan evidence, not memory. I take my last three specs, my roadmap with its outcome measures, my mock or review scorecards and my stories, and check each against a list of detection signals: for example, does each roadmap item have a metric, did I state the guardrail, did the story show a decision. I count how often each pattern appears, group them by root cause (activity mistaken for progress, optimizing the measure, avoiding discomfort, pattern-matching, not closing the loop), and look for the dominant cluster. &quot;Then one practice, not five: say, 'I name the guardrail in the same sentence as the metric.' I apply it to the next artifacts and recheck in three or four weeks. I also ask a teammate for one example of each pattern they've seen me do, since I can't see all of them. Last time my scan showed that in 6 of 8 mocks I gave no guardrail; after the practice and ten drills, it was 1 in 4.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence; counts; a cluster; one practice and a retest; outside views.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A product has 40 million registered users, but most sessions are very short. Is it healthy?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;40 million users is great.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Questions the registered number and asks about engagement.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes how mean and median differ, defines meaningful use, asks how sessions arise (intent or incidental), reads cohorts and retention, and sets decision criteria.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Not necessarily. Registered users counts everyone who ever signed up and says nothing about use. If 90% of sessions last about 3 seconds and 10% last about 300, the mean is 32.7 seconds, which looks fine, but the median is 3 seconds and 92% of the time comes from 10% of sessions. So I'd measure the median and the share of users with repeated meaningful sessions, say three sessions of at least 30 seconds a week, and retention by cohort. &quot;I'd ask how short sessions arise: from users who intend to be there, or from links, notifications or integrations in other products that land them there? Incidental sessions are not interest. I'd also check who the long sessions are: a loyal minority is a possible niche; no one is a problem. Decision criteria: if fewer than, say, a tenth of registered users meet the engagement bar and cohort retention falls to near zero, the issue is not growth but a missing reason to return, and I'd narrow to the segment with real use or stop.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Distribution; meaningful use; origin of sessions; cohorts; a decision rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c29-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C29-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C29 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You discover your team has been optimizing a metric that does not matter for the last six months. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Switch metrics and move on.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Tells the team and leadership and resets the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Verifies the finding, owns your part, quantifies what was lost, tells stakeholders early with a plan, reconstructs which work still matters, sets the right metric with a guardrail and a review date, and changes the practice that allowed it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;First verify: what exactly doesn't matter, and what is the evidence (no relation to retention or revenue in cohorts, say). Then tell my manager and the team promptly, in person and then in writing: here is what we found, what it cost (say, six months of two-thirds of our effort), what I think we should do and when I'll update. I'd own my part, since I helped set the goal, without self-flagellation. &quot;Then reset: choose the metric that tracks value (a retained-user measure, with a guardrail), set a baseline and a target, and review the past six months of work against it to see what still helps: some of it likely does. Decide what to stop, keep and reshape, with numbers. And I'd change the practice: every goal metric gets a documented link to an outcome, checked quarterly against cohorts, so this can't recur silently.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Verification; early disclosure with a plan; ownership; salvage; a changed practice.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c30-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C30-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C30 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you take a new product idea from discovery to launch?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Research, design, build, launch.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Stages with evidence and a test before the build.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the riskiest assumption, a cheap test with thresholds set first, a spec with non-goals, a metric tree with guardrails, a staged rollout and a review after launch.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd stage it so that spend follows evidence. Discovery: understand the user and the problem with interviews, observation and data, and list the assumptions; the riskiest is usually that people will pay or change behavior. Alpha: test that assumption with the cheapest experiment that can fail, such as a smoke test or a manual version, with the thresholds written first (proceed, stop, and what a middle result triggers). Spec: a short document with the problem statement, goals, non-goals and acceptance criteria. Metrics: a tree with a primary measure, the drivers and a guardrail. Beta: a staged rollout, private then wider, with a rollback trigger. Live: a review at a set date against the success criteria, and a retrospective that records the pattern and what I'd change. &quot;At each gate I'd decide to proceed, persist, pivot or stop by the rules written before the evidence arrived.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence-gated stages; thresholds first; non-goals; a review.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c30-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C30-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C30 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is the difference between discovery, alpha, beta and live?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Early, middle and late stages.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> The purpose of each.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States the evidence needed to exit each phase, the decision at each gate and that the live phase keeps reviewing; notes that public-sector digital teams use this model.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Discovery is for understanding users, constraints and the problem; its exit is evidence of the problem, the riskiest assumptions and a plan for testing them. Alpha is for testing different solutions to those assumptions, usually with prototypes; its exit is a tested solution with results against thresholds. Beta builds the best option and tests it with real users, first a limited private group and then everyone; its exit is a working end-to-end service with metrics, guardrails and operational readiness. Live runs and keeps improving the service, with reviews of outcomes and a plan for the next cycle. Public-sector digital teams, such as the UK government's, publish this four-phase model with assessments at gates, and it maps well onto product work.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Purpose and exit evidence for each; gates; continued review.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c30-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C30-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product-sense · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C30 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You are building a learning app for students in a market with low-end phones and weak data. What goes into the first version?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Videos and quizzes.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts from the job and constraints.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the riskiest assumptions (trust and willingness to pay), tests them manually first, designs for data and device limits, grounds AI answers in the textbook, and sets accuracy criteria by stratum.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd start from the job: students stuck on a topic with no one to ask at night. The riskiest assumptions are that they and their parents will trust the answers and that parents will pay. I'd test that manually before building: a concierge test on a messaging channel for 40 students for four weeks, with drafts reviewed by a teacher, and thresholds written first (for example, at least 90% of reviewed answers correct, at least 3 questions per student per week, at least 10% of parents paying after two free weeks). &quot;First version: answers generated from the textbook passages with the page cited, a fallback to 'ask your teacher' when unsure, offline caching of the last answers, under 3 MB of data a session, and a 95th-percentile response under six seconds on a slow connection. Not in version one: videos, gamification, grade prediction. Accuracy criteria by difficulty stratum, not just an average, since an average can hide a weak chapter. Guardrails: wrong-answer reports, data cost, parent complaints, late-night use.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Job first; riskiest assumptions; manual test; device constraints; stratified accuracy.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c30-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C30-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C30 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you start a marketplace connecting small neighborhood shops with wholesalers?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build an app and onboard both sides.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts in one district and tests manually.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the supply behavior as the riskiest assumption, measures liquidity (acceptance within a time, fill rate, repeat), computes concierge economics, and notes regulatory limits on credit and payments.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd start in one district and run it by hand. The riskiest assumption is supply behavior: will wholesalers accept orders through a platform and keep price lists current? I'd take 30 shops' orders on a messaging app in their language and phone wholesalers myself for six weeks, with thresholds written first: at least 60% of orders accepted within 30 minutes (stop under 50%), at least half the shops ordering three times (stop under 40%). &quot;I'd measure liquidity, not sign-ups: the share of orders accepted within 30 minutes, the fill rate and repeat ordering, with disputes and credit defaults as guardrails. Economics: with a 2% fee on a ₹4,500 order (₹90) against ₹95 of concierge cost and ₹18 of default cost, contribution is negative ₹23 an order, so the automation targets are the manual coordination steps: bringing cost under about ₹35 makes it about ₹37 positive. I'd keep credit to a ledger that only records what wholesalers extend until I've checked the rules on lending and payments, and stay in the district until acceptance holds above 75%.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> One district; liquidity measures; economics; regulatory caution.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c30-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C30-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C30 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your enterprise pilot succeeded at three sites. Why might the deal still stall, and what do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The customer might not have budget.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the buyer, security and integration.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Maps the buying committee, plans the non-feature requirements early, plans for a seven-month sales cycle, prices to the buyer's logic, and sets the pilot's exit criteria to include procurement and security.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;A pilot proves the users like it; the deal needs the economic buyer, security, IT and procurement. It stalls when a security review or a single sign-on or audit-log requirement appears late, when the champion lacks budget authority, or when integration beyond the pilot system is needed. In one project a security review added about ten weeks because the documentation wasn't ready. &quot;So I'd map the buying committee at the start of the pilot (economic buyer, champion, administrators, IT and security, procurement and legal), put the security and compliance work into the plan at the end of discovery, write exit criteria that include procurement and security milestones, and give the buyer a business case in their terms: handling time down 27%, about 0.7 hours a shift saved per supervisor, against $1,800 per site per month (the figures here are from Journey 2 in C30, all invented). I'd plan for the real sales cycle (about seven months) and commit to outcomes and ranges to sales, not dated features.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Buying committee; non-feature requirements early; a business case; realistic cycle.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c30-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C30-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C30 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An agent drafts purchase orders and a person approves each one. The median approval takes three seconds. Is that safe?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, a person approves everything.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Questions whether reviewers really check.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Tests reviewers with seeded errors, redesigns the approval screen to surface changes and uncertainty, tracks time and edits, and ties autonomy to supervised evidence by band with demotion.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Not necessarily: approval time of three seconds suggests many approvals are clicks, and a human in the loop only helps if the human is checking. I'd test it: seed a known set of errors into drafts, say 50, and see how many reviewers catch. In one test they caught 30 of 50 (60%); after changing the screen to highlight changed and low-confidence fields, they caught 41 of 50 (82%). I'd run that test monthly as a standing check. &quot;I'd also track approval time, edits and override rates, not only acceptance. For autonomy, I'd use a ladder: draft-only for everything first; then, for a narrow band such as low-value reorders from an allow-listed vendor, autonomy after 300 supervised cases with zero errors (which bounds the error rate near 1%), and automatic demotion to draft-only on any error. Controls around it: untrusted-text handling against prompt injection, spending caps, an audit log and a kill switch.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Reviewer testing; screen design; time and edits; earned autonomy; controls.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c30-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C30-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> ai · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C30 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You run an internal AI evaluation platform used by 40 product teams. You cannot mandate adoption. How do you drive it, and how do you measure success?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Promote it and track how many teams use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Reduces friction and measures value.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats teams as customers, measures regressions caught rather than activity, keeps time to first run low, calibrates judges, uses narrow mandates only where risk is high, and checks that the metric can't be gamed.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd treat the teams as customers. Interview non-adopters to find the barrier; in one case it was setup time, so I'd cut time to first evaluation run to under a day with a template. I'd show value with results: in an 8-week pilot with 5 teams, 4 ran evaluations weekly and caught 11 regressions, 3 of which would have shipped. I'd calibrate any model-based judge against human labels before offering it (for example, 62.5% recall of bad outputs rose to 77.5% with claim-level checks). &quot;For measures, the headline is regressions caught that would have shipped and coverage of known failure modes, not evaluations run, since teams can inflate that with trivial runs. Adoption goes from 12 of 40 to 25 through templates, office hours and visible wins, with no mandate except for high-risk launches where the risk function requires evidence. Guardrails: reliability, cost per run, support load and team satisfaction. Each quarter I'd check that the headline can't be raised by trivial runs.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Customer view; value measures; calibrated judge; narrow mandate; anti-gaming check.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c30-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C30-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C30 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a product you took from idea to launch.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A chronological description with no numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Evidence, a test and a result.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A through-line with a riskiest assumption, thresholds set first, a spec with non-goals, a decision by the pre-set rule, and a retrospective with a pattern and a changed practice.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>(A composite example; the figures are invented.)</em> &quot;We saw that 38% of new users dropped at a permissions step. I interviewed ten users and found they didn't understand why we needed access, so the riskiest assumption was that a clearer explanation would fix it. We tested a prototype with 12 users, then an A/B test on 5% of traffic with thresholds written first: proceed at a four-point completion gain, stop under one, and a guardrail on support contacts. The spec had two non-goals: no redesign of the whole flow, and no change to the permissions themselves. &quot;At week one the result was in the middle zone (a 2.5-point gain), so I extended the test two weeks rather than moving the threshold, and it reached 4.6 points. We launched in stages; completion rose from 52% to 58% and support contacts were flat. The retrospective found that my first interviews were all active users, which could have hidden why lapsed users never returned; I now include lapsed users in discovery, and in the next project that changed the problem we picked. I can show the metric tree and the spec.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence; riskiest assumption; pre-set thresholds; non-goals; pattern and changed practice.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about yourself.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A chronological recitation of the résumé.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A two-minute arc with scope, steps with reasons and a link to the role.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Plants three specific proof points with numbers that invite probes, shows a motive supported by evidence, and ends with a hand-over.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>(A composite candidate from operations, to show a different path from Worked example 1.)</em> &quot;I'm an associate on the operations analytics team at a logistics start-up, where I own the weekly reporting on delivery delays for about 12,000 deliveries a week. I got here in three steps. I started in customer support for a food-delivery company, where I noticed the same five complaints every week and began tagging them; that tagging showed that 31% of contacts were about one confusing status message. I wrote up the finding and the product team changed the message, and contacts about it fell by about half. That was the first time I saw how one decision could change a lot of people's day, and it made me want to be the person making it. I then moved to analytics, where I built the delay report and found that two warehouses caused 40% of late deliveries; I proposed a change to cutoff times that the operations lead tried, and late deliveries at those sites fell from 14% to 9%. Most recently I've started running small experiments on notification wording with our product team. What I want now is to own a product area, and your team's work on delivery tracking is a problem I know from the support side and from the data side. I'm happy to go deeper on any of those.&quot; About 230 words. <em>Signals.</em> Scope; an arc with reasons; three proof points (the tagging, the delay report, the experiments); a motive with evidence; a hand-over.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Why product management, and why this company and role?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I like solving problems and working with people; your company is the leader in its field.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives evidence for the motive and one or two concrete reasons for the company.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows the realities of the work have been understood, connects a specific company fact to a specific personal fact, and offers what the candidate would examine first in the role.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Two things pulled me to product. One: in my current role I kept being the person who found the problem and then had to hand it to someone else to decide, and I realized I wanted the deciding part: what to build, for whom, and what not to build. Two: the work I enjoy most is the unglamorous part, like deciding which of three reasonable requests gets the one engineer. In a recent quarter I had to say no to two stakeholders and wrote them each a short note with the reasoning; I found I liked the discipline of that. I also know what I would be giving up: less time for analysis and more time in conversations I can't always win. &quot;For this company and role: I use your product weekly, and I've tried the new sharing flow, which I think handles a real problem, the awkward step of sending to someone who has no account, in a way that most of your competitors do not. I've also read your published principles about putting users first, and my own best work, the status-message change, came from reading user complaints, not dashboards. For this role, I would want to understand first how new users reach their first shared item, because that is where I'd expect the biggest gap.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Evidence for the motive; awareness of the costs of the job; a specific, honest reason for the company; a first question for the role.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a time you disagreed with an engineer about scope.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;We disagreed and then compromised.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Context, the decision, an action showing listening and a result.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Represents the engineer's view fairly, finds a cheaper test, credits their idea, states a result with a number and a behavioral learning.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Notes.</em> Context: a feature to let users export their data; I wanted export in three formats at launch; the engineer said one format would take two weeks and three would take seven; the launch was tied to a partner event in five weeks (invented). Decision: insist on three formats, accept one, or find out which formats mattered. Action: I asked what drove the cost (conversion code for two formats); I checked with support and found that 86% of export requests last quarter were for one format; I proposed launching with that format and a request form for the others; the engineer suggested making the form collect which format and why, which gave us data. Result: launched in two and a half weeks; the form collected 140 requests in a month, of which 61% asked for one second format, which we built next; no missed event. Learning: ask what drives the cost, and check who actually needs each option, before arguing about scope. <em>Told (about 1 minute).</em> &quot;We were building a data export feature. I wanted three formats at launch; the engineer said one would take two weeks and three would take seven, and we had five weeks until a partner event. I could have insisted, accepted his view, or tried to learn what mattered. I asked what made the extra formats expensive, which was conversion code, and then I checked support's tickets: 86% of export requests were for a single format. So I proposed launching with that one and a request form for the rest, and he suggested the form ask which format and why, so we'd learn. We launched in two and a half weeks. In a month the form had 140 requests, 61% for one other format, which we built next. What I took away is to ask what drives the cost and who really needs each option before debating scope.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A fair account of his view; a decision to learn; a number-backed resolution; shared credit.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a time you disagreed with a decision and committed anyway.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I didn't agree, but I did what my manager asked.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the disagreement with evidence, voices it, and commits fully after the decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows candid advocacy before the decision, wholehearted execution after it, a protective action (monitoring or a reversal condition), an honest result including that the decision was right or wrong, and learning.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Notes.</em> Context: my manager decided to launch a redesigned pricing page to everyone without a test, to meet a quarter-end date; I had seen two earlier redesigns lower conversion (invented). Decision: argue until overruled, then commit with a safeguard, or quietly disengage. Action: I wrote a one-page note with the two earlier results, a proposal for a 10% holdback for two weeks at a cost of about 2% of the page's quarterly volume, and the risk if the page was worse (a month at a lower rate, about $90,000 in lost revenue); my manager read it, weighed the quarter-end date, and decided to launch to everyone. I said I disagreed and would commit. I then built the launch plan, set up a daily conversion check with a threshold for rollback, and told the team the decision was made and why. Result: conversion on the new page was 3% lower in week one; we hit the threshold on day five, rolled back and shipped a revised page two weeks later that tested 4% higher. The cost was about a week at the lower rate. Learning: I was right about the risk, and my manager was right that the date mattered, so I now bring the date's cost in numbers to the first conversation; and I protect a decision I disagree with by agreeing the rollback condition in advance. <em>Told (about 1 minute 35 seconds).</em> &quot;My manager decided to launch a redesigned pricing page to everyone, without a test, to hit a quarter-end date. I disagreed: I had seen two earlier redesigns lower conversion. I could argue until I was overruled, commit with a safeguard, or quietly disengage. Before the decision I wrote a one-page note with the two earlier results and a proposal to hold back 10% of traffic for two weeks, which would cost about 2% of the page's quarterly volume; if the page was worse we would lose about $90,000 over a month. My manager weighed the date and decided to launch to everyone. I said I still disagreed and that I would commit, and I did: I wrote the launch plan, set up a daily conversion check with an agreed rollback threshold, and told the team the decision was made and why. In week one conversion was 3% lower; we hit the threshold on day five, rolled back, and shipped a revised page two weeks later that tested 4% higher. The cost was about a week at the lower rate. I was right about the risk and my manager was right that the date mattered, so now I bring the date's cost in numbers to the first conversation, and I agree a rollback condition in advance for any decision I disagree with.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Candor before the decision, commitment after, a protective mechanism, honest accounting.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a product detail you obsessed over, and what it cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'm a perfectionist; I care about every detail.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A specific detail, why it mattered to users, and a named cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows judgment about which detail was worth the cost, quantifies both, admits where the obsession was or wasn't justified, and shows how the candidate now decides what to obsess over.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Notes.</em> Context: the empty state of a new user's first screen in a note-taking app: a blank page with a cursor (invented). Users who landed there had a 46% chance of leaving without writing anything. Decision: ship the planned release on time with the blank screen, or spend time on the first-run experience. Action: I watched ten new users start (six stared at the blank page); I tried 12 variants of the first line and an example note; two engineers and a designer spent five days on it, which pushed a smaller sharing improvement to the next release. The cost was a delay of that improvement by two weeks, five person-days and a lot of my time on wording. Result: first-session note creation rose from 54% to 63%, and support contacts about &quot;how to start&quot; fell by about 18%. Not everything I obsessed over paid off: I spent a day on an animation that tests showed no one noticed, and I cut it. Learning: I now decide what to obsess over by asking where the user's attention is and where the drop-off is; the detail has to sit on the path that matters. <em>Signals.</em> Observation of users; a priced tradeoff; honesty about waste; a rule for choosing.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Describe a time you protected user privacy against a business request.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I told them it was wrong and they agreed.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the request, the harm, the alternative offered and the outcome.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Applies a clear frame (facts, harms, goal behind the request, options), proposes a route to the legitimate goal, accepts and states a real cost, escalates properly, and reflects honestly.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Notes (FW-C31-08).</em> Context: a partner team asked me to share a list of 40,000 users' email addresses with a marketing partner for a co-promotion, expecting about $60,000 in revenue (invented). Users had not agreed to partner marketing. Facts: I checked our privacy policy and consent records; the policy did not cover it. Harm: users' addresses shared without consent; a trust and legal risk. Goal behind the request: reach likely customers. Options: share anyway, refuse, or offer an alternative. Decision: offer a consent-based version and refuse the list. Action: I proposed an in-product message to our users offering the partner's promotion with an opt-in, and a matched audience measured in aggregate; I wrote this up for the product lead and the privacy owner; the partner team was unhappy: the opt-in route would take three weeks and was expected to reach about 15% of the list. Result: the opt-in drew about 6,200 users, produced about $11,000 in the first month and no complaints; the original plan was dropped. Cost: three weeks' delay, about 80% less revenue in the short term, and some frustration with me. Learning: bring an alternative that serves the goal, not only an objection, and get the privacy owner involved early. <em>Signals.</em> A frame applied; a legitimate goal respected; a priced cost; a proper escalation.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a time you worked on an ambiguous problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The goal wasn't clear, so I asked questions until it was.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Shows how the candidate structured the problem and moved forward.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States assumptions, uses cheap tests, narrows with evidence, brings stakeholders along, decides what to ignore, and shows what changed as understanding improved.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Notes.</em> Context: I was asked to &quot;improve activation&quot; for a small-business invoicing tool with no agreed definition of activation and three stakeholders with three ideas (invented). Decision: pick an idea, or define the problem first. Action: I proposed a working definition (a user who sends one invoice within seven days), checked it against retention (users who sent an invoice in week one were 3.1 times more likely to be active at day 60); listed three hypotheses for why users did not send one (they did not know how, they had no customer ready, they feared mistakes); ran a cheap test of each: a five-user observation (for the first), a survey of 200 non-senders (for the second), and a &quot;preview invoice&quot; prototype (for the third); the survey showed that 54% of non-senders had no customer ready, which surprised the stakeholders who had assumed the product was too hard; I agreed with them to test a &quot;sample invoice&quot; feature first. I deliberately set aside two ideas (a rewards program and a redesigned dashboard) with the reasons written down. Result: sample invoice use rose and seven-day activation went from 22% to 27%. Learning: define the term with data before choosing a solution, and write down what you are setting aside and why. <em>Signals.</em> Structure under ambiguity; assumptions made explicit; cheap tests; stakeholders involved; discipline in what to ignore.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a time you moved fast and it went wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I worked quickly, a bug got through, and I fixed it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the tradeoff made, the failure, the repair and the lesson.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows why speed seemed right, quantifies the damage and the response time, owns the decision, and shows a lasting practice that preserves speed.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Notes.</em> Context: a one-line change to the sign-up form that I believed was safe, shipped on a Friday afternoon to hit a weekly goal (invented). I skipped the usual staged rollout. Decision: wait until Monday and ramp, or ship now. Why speed seemed right: the change was tiny, the team had a streak of weekly releases, and nothing in the test environment failed. What happened: for users on one older mobile browser (about 7% of sign-ups) the form's button stopped working; sign-ups from that group fell to near zero for 90 minutes before support noticed; about 1,100 users could not sign up. Repair: I rolled the change back within 20 minutes of the first report, wrote to the support team and affected users, and added an automatic alert for sign-up drops by browser. Cost: about 1,100 failed sign-ups, a night of the team's attention and some trust. Learning: the speed I valued was real, and what protected it was a staged rollout behind a flag (1%, then 10%) and an alert; since then every change to sign-up ships to 1% first, and releases still go weekly. I own that I skipped the staging to hit a streak. <em>Signals.</em> Honest tradeoff; quantified damage; ownership; a practice that keeps speed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a time you gave candid feedback to someone more senior than you.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I told my director I disagreed, and he appreciated it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Specific feedback, delivered thoughtfully, with an outcome.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows preparation and respect (private, specific, evidence, a proposal), handles a negative reaction, accepts the cost, and shows a pattern of candor with care.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Notes.</em> Context: in a planning review, a director presented a slide saying a feature had &quot;raised retention by 8%,&quot; based on a comparison of users who used the feature with those who did not (invented). I believed it overstated the effect because heavier users were more likely to use it. Decision: say nothing, raise it in the meeting, or talk to the director privately. Action: I looked at the data first and found that after matching users by prior activity, the lift was about 2%; I asked for 15 minutes the same day, said that I wanted to check my reading of the slide before the next review, showed the matched comparison and said that the 8% was probably selection; I proposed a holdout test and offered to run the analysis. The director was irritated at first (&quot;this was already in the deck&quot;) and then asked for the analysis; the slide was corrected before it went to the executive team. Cost: some tension that week, and a colleague told me later that I should have mentioned it before the meeting. Learning: next time I raise concerns with data before a meeting, not after, and I always bring a proposed fix. We have worked well since, and he asks for my review of numbers before he presents. <em>Signals.</em> Candor with care; preparation; a proposal; accepting the cost; a good relationship afterward.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a time you changed your mind about a product decision because of new information.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I thought one thing and then learned another, so I changed.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the original belief, the new evidence, the change and its effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows genuine attachment to the original view, how the candidate sought disconfirming evidence, how the change was communicated and what it says about the candidate's learning habits.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Notes.</em> Context: I was convinced that power users wanted advanced filters in a reporting tool, because they were the loudest in feedback and our top accounts were power users (invented). I had written the spec and told my manager the feature would be the quarter's headline. Decision: build as planned, or test the assumption. New information: before the build, I ran six interviews with newer users to check a different question and found that four of six could not find the basic filter at all; usage data then showed that 63% of new accounts never applied any filter in the first month. Action: I told my manager that the evidence contradicted my plan and showed both sets of numbers; we agreed to build a simpler first-use filter flow first and defer advanced filters; I told the power users why, and offered them an early look at the advanced version later. Result: filter use in the first month rose from 37% to 58% of new accounts; the advanced filters shipped one quarter later and were used by 11% of accounts, which was lower than I had expected. Learning: the loudest users are not the largest group, and I now ask for evidence that would contradict my belief before I commit a spec, and I check how many users each request represents. <em>Signals.</em> Real attachment to the first view; seeking disconfirming evidence; clean communication of the change; a durable habit.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-11\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-11</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A launch is tomorrow, and you discover a defect that corrupts the export for about 3% of users. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd delay the launch,&quot; or &quot;I'd launch and fix it later.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Clarifies, weighs the harm and the cost of delay, and chooses.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the situational structure and the values frame: facts first, harm and reversibility, options such as a partial launch or disabling the feature, honest communication, a decision with a cost, and how it would be checked.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Clarify (two or three questions).</em> How sure are we of the 3% and what does corrupts mean: lost data, or a malformed file? Can affected users recover (do they still have the source data)? Is the export the headline of the launch or one feature among many? Who decides, and how fast can it be fixed? Suppose the answers are: the 3% is from a test of 2,000 accounts; the source data stays intact, so users can export again after a fix; and the export is one of six features. <em>Principle.</em> Do not ship a known data-corruption defect to users without their knowing; users' trust in their data is what a data product sells. <em>Options and tradeoffs.</em> (A) Delay the whole launch until the fix: the safest and costs the launch event. (B) Launch with the export feature turned off behind a flag for everyone, and enable it when fixed: keeps most of the launch and loses one feature. (C) Launch with the export on and a warning: fast and exposes 3% of users to corrupt files, which they may not notice. (D) Launch on schedule to a small group first. <em>First action.</em> I would choose B if the flag exists and the other five features are not affected: the harm is irreversible only if users act on corrupt files, and turning the feature off prevents that. I would tell the launch owner and support tonight, with the facts and the plan; ask engineering for a fix estimate and a test that covers the failing case; and prepare the message for users (&quot;export will arrive next week; here's how to request it now&quot;). If no flag exists and the export is central, I would recommend delaying the launch by the time to the fix, and say plainly what that costs. <em>Check.</em> Re-test with the failing accounts; confirm the flag state in production; watch export-related contacts; hold a short review afterward on how the defect was missed, and add the case to the test suite. <em>Cost I accept.</em> A launch without a feature the partners expected, a harder conversation with the launch owner, and possibly being seen as the person who made it smaller.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c31-12\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C31-12</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C31 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Tell me about a time you worked with a teammate who was not delivering.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;They were slow, so I did their work,&quot; or &quot;I told my manager.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Describes understanding the cause, a direct conversation and a result.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Starts with curiosity about the cause, clarifies expectations in writing, offers specific help, brings in the manager with facts and the person's knowledge, protects the outcome and the relationship, and ends with a measurable result and a lesson.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Context (invented figures).</em> On a team of six I was the associate product manager for a checkout change, and a design colleague was consistently missing the dates we had agreed: in the first four weeks she delivered 2 of 5 committed pieces on time (40%). The launch was six weeks out. <em>Decision.</em> I could take over her pieces, escalate to her manager, or find out why. I decided to start with a private conversation, because I didn't know the cause and assumed it might not be effort. <em>Action.</em> I asked a neutral question: &quot;I've noticed the last few dates slipped, and I want to make sure I'm giving you what you need. What's getting in the way?&quot; She told me she was supporting two other projects with competing deadlines and that my requests arrived as changing priorities in chat. We agreed three things: I would send a single written brief for each piece with the acceptance criteria and a fixed date; we would hold a 15-minute check-in twice a week; and I would tell her manager, with her knowledge, that the team's design capacity was over-committed so that the priorities could be set at their level. I also moved two lower-value pieces out of the launch scope. <em>Result.</em> In the next four weeks she delivered 9 of 10 pieces on time (90%), the launch shipped on its date with reduced scope, and her manager reduced her other commitments. She told me later that the written briefs were the most useful change. <em>Learning.</em> The cause was a system problem (conflicting priorities and unclear requests) that looked like a person problem. I now check the cause before concluding, write requests down with criteria, and raise capacity problems with the manager as facts and not as complaints about a person. <em>Signals.</em> Curiosity first; a direct, kind conversation; specific commitments; the manager informed with the person's knowledge; a measured result; a lesson.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Summarize a complex product decision for a non-technical executive in sixty seconds.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Starts with the history and technical background; the decision comes late.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the decision and the main reason first, with a number.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Answer first, two or three named supports in plain language, the risk and the ask, and a closing restatement, within the time.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;We decided to add a second payment provider, and I need your approval to start next week. Three reasons. First, reliability: our single provider failed for four hours last quarter and we lost about $180,000 in sales. Second, cost: a second provider lets us negotiate, and we estimate a 10 to 15% fall in processing fees, about $300,000 a year. Third, speed: the work is six engineer-weeks, which fits this quarter. The main risk is a more complex checkout, which we'd mitigate with a staged rollout to 10% of customers first. So: add the second provider, start next week, rollout in stages. Can I have your approval today?&quot; (about 100 words; about 45 seconds). <em>Signals.</em> Answer and ask first; three named reasons in plain terms, each with a number; the risk with a mitigation; the ask repeated.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you structure a weekly update to your manager?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I list what I did this week.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Status, what changed, what's next and what's needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Headline with status and the ask, news with impact (not activity), an explicit &quot;need from you&quot; with a deadline, brevity, and a link for the detail.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Four lines, headline first. The headline gives status and whether I need anything: 'On track for the 12th; one decision needed.' Then <em>what changed</em> since last week, with the impact: not 'met with legal' but 'legal flagged a consent wording issue; fix is a one-line copy change.' Then <em>what's next</em>, two items. Then <em>what I need from you</em>, with a deadline: 'Approve the copy change by Monday.' If I need nothing, I say so, since 'nothing needed' is useful news. I keep it under 80 words and link a running log for detail, so my manager can stop reading after the headline if all is well.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Reader's time respected; news not activity; an explicit ask; honesty about status.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you get an engineer to build something they don't want to build?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I explain why it's important until they agree.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Understands the engineer's concern and finds a shared goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the ladder (incentives, shared goal, evidence, pre-wire, clear small ask, credit), trades scope openly, accepts a no that is right, and keeps the relationship.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I start by finding out why they don't want to. It is usually a real reason: risk to the system, maintenance burden, a better use of their time, or distrust that the priority is real. So I ask, 'What worries you about this?' and listen. Then I look for the goal we share: if the feature is to cut failed payments, I show the numbers (3% of payments fail, costing about $40,000 a month) and ask how they'd solve it, since their way may be better than my feature. I offer to trade scope: 'If we cut the report export, does the rest fit in two weeks?' I make the ask small and concrete: 'Can you spend half a day to estimate it and tell me what would make it risky?' If their answer is that it's a bad idea, I take it seriously and ask what evidence would change their mind; sometimes I am wrong. If we still disagree, I say who decides, which is me for what, and they flag the risk in writing. And I give credit: when it ships, I say whose idea made it safer.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Listening; shared goal; evidence with limits; trade of scope; respect; credit.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-04</h3>\n";
